00:24:53.625 --> 00:24:59.976
From Microsoft Developer Blogs: Do inconsistent docs hurt AI coding agents?

00:25:00.081 --> 00:25:05.545
Every product’s docs drift out of step with the product. A product that’s been

00:25:05.545 --> 00:25:10.739
around for years has pages written against features that have since changed, and each

00:25:10.739 --> 00:25:17.323
change reaches only the pages someone remembers to update. A product that’s moving fast,

00:25:17.323 --> 00:25:23.281
as many are today with AI, drifts even sooner. An endpoint gets renamed or

00:25:23.281 --> 00:25:27.726
a setting moves, and the reference gets updated while a how-to or a sample

00:25:27.726 --> 00:25:33.337
keeps the old value. Before long, two of your pages tell the reader two

00:25:33.337 --> 00:25:39.837
different things. When developers hit two pages that disagree, they usually find out which

00:25:39.837 --> 00:25:44.767
one is right the hard way. They copy the code from one page, run

00:25:44.767 --> 00:25:47.016
it and watch it fail.

00:25:47.121 --> 00:25:49.008
